From 300a4fcd8403656f6d235ebe62a5e640dc2843b1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sridhar Kumar Ettkepalli <SridharKumar.Ettkepalli@windriver.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 23:47:27 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Clear maintenance on a host recovered by Masakari

When Masakari starts recovering a failed host it sets the host's
on_maintenance flag, and nothing ever clears it. The API then rejects
every later notification from a host in maintenance with 409, so once
a host has been recovered and is back in service it is no longer
protected: its next failure is dropped.

Have the Kubernetes host monitor clear the flag once the host is back.
Each monitor keeps a set of hosts to check: every node when it starts,
and any node that goes down. When such a host is Ready for the whole
sample window, the monitor looks the hosts up in one segment listing per
poll and checks the host's latest COMPUTE_HOST notification first, by
creation time, since a new notification has no update time yet. While it
is new, running or error the engine may still put the host in
maintenance, so the monitor checks again on the next poll. The flag is
cleared only when Masakari set it: the notification is finished or
failed and the host was not updated after it. A flag set by an operator
is left alone, including one behind an ignored notification. A 409 while
another notification in the segment is processed, or an unreachable API,
is retried on the next poll. A monitor never acts on its own host.

A config option (restore_recovered_hosts, default False) turns this on.
The nova-compute service is not touched here: the platform re-enables
it once the host is back.

Relates-to: https://review.opendev.org/c/openstack/masakari-monitors/+/1016324
Signed-off-by: Sridhar Kumar Ettkepalli <SridharKumar.Ettkepalli@windriver.com>
---
